{
  "source": "research:mainland-sea",
  "note": "Burmese, Cambodian, Lao and Bruneian dishes — the mainland Southeast Asian repertoires the earlier country rounds did not reach. WEB SEARCH WAS UNAVAILABLE FOR THIS ROUND: every figure is a model estimate reasoned from the dish's own ingredients and anchored against comparable rows the catalogue already holds (the Thai round's noodle bowls and som tam, the Vietnamese round's phở and bánh mì, the Malaysian nasi and mee rows, the MyFCD component rows). Nothing was read off a published table, so every row is verified:false and source:model_estimate. Brunei is deliberately thin: Bruneian and Malaysian cooking are close to the same repertoire and the catalogue already holds Ambuyat, Cacah Binjai, Cacah Tempoyak, Linut, Nasi Katok, Kuih Kelupis, Kuih Pinjaram, Kuih Cincin, Kuih Jala, Kuih Tahi Itik, Wajid Sabah, Pulut Panggang, Bubur Pedas, Air Katira and Sagol, so only what is genuinely Brunei's own is written here and the rest was widened with foods:alias. Where a name collides with a Thai or Vietnamese row already in the catalogue the country leads the name (Lao Larb Moo against Larb Moo, Lao Khao Soi against Khao Soi Gai). Portions are the local street portion: a Burmese mohinga is the standard bowl, a danbauk is one plate, a Lao sticky rice basket is the one that comes with the meal, and a Khmer num pang is one 20 cm baguette.",
